div.p:
3. But it is not these of whom the Apostle is here speaking, neither is this the treasure which is the subject of his discourse. The persons concerning whom he is here speaking are those that are born of God, those that, ‘being justified by faith’,
Rom. 5:1. have now ‘redemption in the blood of Jesus, even the forgiveness of sins’;
Cf. Col. 1:14. those who enjoy that peace of God which passeth all understanding;
Phil. 4:7. whose soul doth magnify the Lord,
See Luke 1:46 (Magnificat). and rejoice in him with joy unspeakable;
1 Pet. 1:8. and who feel ‘the love of God shed abroad in their hearts by the Holy Ghost, which is given unto them’.
Cf. Rom. 5:5. This then is the treasure which they have received, a faith of the operation of God,
Col. 2:12. a peace which sets them above the fear of death, and enables them in everything to be content;
See Phil. 4:11. an hope full of immortality,
Wisd. 3:4; see No. 72, ‘Of Evil Angels’, II.4 and n. whereby they already ‘taste of the powers of the world to come’;
Heb. 6:5. the love of God shed abroad in their hearts, with love to every child of man, and a renewal in the whole image of God, in all righteousness and true holiness. This is properly and directly the treasure concerning which the Apostle is here speaking.
